        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objective of this study was to determine how acute changes in gravitational force affect the human immune system.  Specifically, this research aimed to assess physical and psychological stress levels, determine the competence of the immune system and measure the reactivation of latent viruses in response to simulated space flight. In previous flight research, elevated levels of stress hormones were found after launch, during space flight and after landing.  Significant increases in stress hormones were directly correlated with reactivation of Epstein-Barr virus and cytomegalovirus, two medically significant herpesviruses.  Space flight-induced viral reactivation may be a result of both direct (i.e., stress hormones) and indirect (i.e., decreased immune function) mechanisms activated by launch and landing accelerations. 
<p>
APPROACH: <br>
Ten subjects underwent realistic launch G-force profiles followed by 16 days of six degree head-down tilt bed rest. Subjects were exposed to an episode of brief hypergravity, a period of extended head-down tilt bed rest, and a second brief episode of hypergravity.  This simulation more precisely mimicked the forces to which astronauts are exposed during a space mission. Blood, urine and saliva samples were collected before, during, and after simulated space flight.  The presence and amount of stress hormones and regulatory cytokines was measured, and changes in leukocyte and lymphocyte subpopulations were assessed.  The number and function of virus-specific T-lymphocytes, as well as viral load, were determined.  Additionally, psychological stress was assessed through the use of standardized questionnaires. 
<p>
RESULTS: <br>
Similar to shorter Shuttle missions, plasma cortisol was significantly decreased at simulated landing while urinary epinephrine was significantly increased. Urinary cortisol was significantly increased after simulated launch. The pattern of leukocyte and lymphocyte changes also mirrored the changes found in shorter nine-day space flights.
<p>
        </div>

